Title: How can I convert the &quot;arguments&quot; object to an array in JavaScript?.
Body: <p>The <code>arguments</code> object in JavaScript is an odd wart—it acts just like an array in most situations, but it's not <em>actually</em> an array object. Since it's <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/arguments" rel="noreferrer">really something else entirely</a>, it doesn't have the useful functions from <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/prototype" rel="noreferrer"><code>Array.prototype</code></a> like <code>forEach</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>filter</code>, and <code>map</code>.</p>

<p>It's trivially easy to construct a new array from an arguments object with a simple for loop. For example, this function sorts its arguments:</p>

<pre><code>function sortArgs() {
    var args = [];
    for (var i = 0; i &lt; arguments.length; i++)
        args[i] = arguments[i];
    return args.sort();
}
</code></pre>

<p>However, this is a rather pitiful thing to have to do simply to get access to the extremely useful JavaScript array functions. Is there a built-in way to do it using the standard library?</p>

