<p>What I am getting is a single trigger of the watch, which I think is happening because the .fieldcontainer = ... assignment takes place immediately after I create my watch. I was thinking of just using a "dirtyCount" property to absorb the initial false alarm but that feels very hacky ... and I figured there has to be an "Angular idiomatic" way to deal with this - I'm not the only one using a watch to detect a dirty model.</p>

<p>Here's the code where I set my watch:</p>

<pre><code> $scope.fieldcontainer = Message.get({id: $scope.entityId },
            function(message,headers) {
