Title: Redirecting to previous page after authentication in node.js using passport.js.
Body: <p>I'm trying to establish a login mechanism using node.js, express and passport.js. The Login itself works quite nice, also sessions are stored nicely with redis but I do have some troubles with redirecting the user to where he started from before being prompted to authenticate.</p>

<p>e.g. User follows link <code>http://localhost:3000/hidden</code> is then redirected to <code>http://localhost:3000/login</code> but then I want him to be redirected again back to <code>http://localhost:3000/hidden</code>.</p>

<p>The purpose of this is, if the user access randomly a page he needs to be logged in first, he shall be redirected to the /login site providing his credentials and then being redirected back to the site he previously tried to access.</p>

<p>Here is my login post</p>

<pre><code>app.post('/login', function (req, res, next) {
    passport.authenticate('local', function (err, user, info) {
        if (err) {
            return next(err)
        } else if (!user) { 
            console.log('message: ' + info.message);
            return res.redirect('/login') 
        } else {
            req.logIn(user, function (err) {
                if (err) {
                    return next(err);
                }
                return next(); // &lt;-? Is this line right?
            });
