Title: Why does Solidity suggest me to implement a receive ether function when I have a fallback function?.
Body: <p>The recent change in Solidity changed the fallback function format from just function() to fallback(), which is pretty nice for beginners to understand what is going on, but I have a question about a suggestion that the compiler gives me when I implement such a fallback.</p>

<p>For example, a piece of code from my project:</p>

<pre><code>pragma solidity ^0.6.1;

contract payment{
    mapping(address =&gt; uint) _balance;

    fallback() payable external {
        _balance[msg.sender] += msg.value;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Everything goes fine, but the compiler suggests that:</p>

<pre><code>Warning: This contract has a payable fallback function, but no receive ether function.
Consider adding a receive ether function.
</code></pre>

<p>What does it mean by a receive ether function? I tried looking it up and many examples I could find is just another fallback function.</p>

